    "summary": "Categories persist, tools change. Learn to map new tools into functional categories.",
    "content": "> [!note] The Course Deliberately Does Not Tie Competence to a Permanent List of Tools.\n> Tools change. Categories persist. Learn the categories. Map new tools onto them.\n\n## Visual Categories\n\nUnderstand categories such as:\n\n- image generators\n- node-based workflow systems\n- structural conditioning\n- identity conditioning\n- image-to-image systems\n- masking and compositing\n- digital drawing / painting\n- reference management\n- versioning\n\nNamed tools are selected according to current availability, learning goals, and your hardware and budget.\n\n> [!example] Mapping a New Tool\n>\n> When a new image generation tool appears, ask:\n> - Is it a generator, a conditioner, an editor, or a pipeline system?\n> - What variables does it control?\n> - What variables does it hide?\n> - What does it do better than my current stack?\n> - What does it do worse?\n> - Can I export my work if it disappears?\n>\n> The answers tell you where it fits — or whether it fits at all.\n\n## Writing Categories\n\n- general language models\n- source-grounded research tools\n- document editors\n- version control\n- outlining systems\n- reference management\n- fact verification systems\n\n## Time-Based, Spatial, and Performative Categories (Deepening)\n\n- audio editors\n- video editors\n- sequencing and score tools\n- spatial modelling or mapping tools\n- lighting and material simulation\n\n## Organization\n\nA usable practice requires:\n\n- project folders\n- version naming\n- reference indexing\n- decision logs\n- anchor libraries\n- problem-finding log\n- non-instrumental attention log\n- calibration log\n- provenance records\n- backups\n\n> [!tip] Keep It Simple\n> You do not need ten logs. Start with three: practice log, problem-finding log, decision log. Add others only when you feel their absence.\n\n## Tool Discipline\n\nThe course tests **conceptual portability**.\n\n> [!warning] A Student Who Can Only Perform a Task Inside One Application Has Tool Fluency, Not Necessarily Transferable Competence.\n\n### Tool-Switch Test\n\nAt least once during the intensive, move a workflow to a different tool category or perform the underlying operation manually.\n\nRecord what changed and what did not.\n\n## Cost, Data Hygiene, and Tool Churn\n\n| Concern | Practice |\n|---|---|\n| Cost | Track monthly spend. Have a free fallback for every paid tool. |\n| Data privacy | Know what your tool does with your inputs. Do not upload confidential or sensitive material without consent. |\n| Model updates | When a model changes, re-run one anchor project and note differences. |\n| Tool sunset | Keep local copies of outputs. Do not depend on a single platform for access to your own work. |\n| Security | Use unique passwords, 2FA, and encrypted storage for client work. |\n| Environmental cost | Be aware of compute cost. Batch where possible. Do not generate wastefully. |\n\n---"
