    "summary": "Universal and contextual rubrics (1-4 scale) and Outcome vs. Decision Quality quadrants.",
    "content": "## Universal Rubric Scale\n\n| Criterion | 4 — Transferable | 3 — Functional | 2 — Emerging | 1 — Not Yet |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Problem-finding | Locates meaningful problems independently and justifies selection | Finds workable problems with some prompting | Notices topics more than problems | Waits for problem to be assigned |\n| Positionality | Situates self honestly and refuses problems not theirs | Usually situates | Rarely considers | Ignores |\n| Non-instrumental attention | Sustains attention without converting it to output | Can do so with effort | Rarely does so | Cannot do so |\n| Observation | Notices relevant structure without prompting | Usually notices | Notices obvious features | Misses key structure |\n| Premise | Generates meaningful premises and selects among them | Generates usable premises | Generates topics more than premises | Waits for premise |\n| Specification | Makes intent operational without overdetermining | Usually operational | Vague or excessive | No useful brief |\n| Diagnosis | Correctly identifies highest unresolved level | Mostly correct | Often local / symptomatic | Surface only |\n| Curation | Uses criteria, reference class, mechanism, purpose | Uses explicit criteria | Preference dominates | No criteria |\n| Systems | Understands variables and failure points | Can run system reliably | Can operate but not explain | Copies blindly |\n| Calibration | Anticipates outcomes with calibrated confidence across wide range | Usually anticipates | Sometimes anticipates | Cannot anticipate |\n| Direction | Makes defensible decisions under constraints | Usually defensible | Seeks excessive reassurance | Avoids decisions |\n| Authorship | Repeated commitments are visible in work | Position is stated and partly demonstrated | Position is mostly verbal | No position |\n| Transfer | Independently applies the framework to unfamiliar problems | Applies with light prompting | Applies only in rehearsed contexts | Cannot transfer |\n| Practice | Designs and maintains own practice; logs evidence | Practices regularly with some structure | Practices sporadically | Waits for assignments |\n| Log integrity | Logs carry specific, non-repetitive thinking | Logs mostly substantive | Logs often perfunctory | Logs are ritual |\n| Play | Regularly makes for no reason; encounters the unexpected | Occasionally plays | Rarely plays | Never plays |\n\n## Contextual Rubric Scale\n\n| Criterion | 4 — Transferable | 3 — Functional | 2 — Emerging | 1 — Not Yet |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Collaboration | Shares authorship and economics without deference or dominance | Usually shares well | Either defers or dominates | Cannot share authorship |\n| Transmission | Scaffolds others without solving, and seeks feedback on teaching | Can scaffold with effort | Struggles to withhold answers | Does the work for them |\n| Public process | Selects and shapes internal artifacts for external work | Can produce public process with prompting | Rarely considers | Does not |\n| Professional practice | Operates inside contracts, scope, pricing, disclosure with clarity | Usually operates well | Struggles with boundaries | Avoids professional framing |\n\n## Two-Axis Assessment\n\nEvery substantial project should be scored on both axes:\n\n| | **Weak outcome** | **Strong outcome** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Weak reasoning** | **Failure** — clearly needs change | **Lucky / unstable** — cannot be reproduced |\n| **Strong reasoning** | **Learning in progress** — execution lags | **Mastery** — target state |\n\n---"
