    "summary": "Not a mood board. A record of decisions worth remembering (≥40% human anchors).",
    "content": "## Purpose\n\nThe anchor library is the external memory of evolving judgment.\n\n> [!important] It Is Not a Mood Board. It Is Not a Collection of Admiration. It Is a Record of **Decisions Worth Remembering**.\n\n## Anchor Record\n\nEach record contains:\n\n- artifact\n- source / provenance\n- medium\n- intended function\n- what succeeds\n- what fails\n- mechanism\n- diagnostic level\n- reference class\n- purpose\n- accept / reject status\n- date added\n- date revisited\n- whether criteria changed\n\n## Comparative Judgment\n\nEvery major addition should be compared to at least two existing anchors.\n\n## Human-Anchor Requirement\n\nAt least 40% of accepted anchors should come from human-created work, not because human work is intrinsically superior, but because you need access to decisions made under conditions not identical to the model's defaults.\n\n## Demotion\n\nAn anchor can be demoted when:\n\n- you discover a stronger example\n- the original judgment depended on novelty\n- the mechanism is inappropriate to the current purpose\n- the artifact is attractive but conceptually thin\n- you realise the criterion was poorly formulated\n\n> [!tip] Demotion Is Progress.\n\n## Versioning\n\nSave snapshots of the library at regular intervals.\n\nCompare old criteria against new criteria.\n\nThe evolution is part of the course.\n\n## Range Audit\n\nEvery quarter, ask whether the library is widening or narrowing.\n\n- Are new anchors structurally different from old ones?\n- Are you adding anchors that surprise you?\n- Are you demoting anchors because your criteria changed, or because you stopped thinking about them?\n\n> [!warning] A Library That Stops Surprising You Has Stopped Calibrating You.\n\n---"
