    "summary": "5-mode refusal system: ethical, positional, technical, intentional, and slop refusal.",
    "content": "> [!important] Refusal Is Part of Direction Because a Creative Director Is Not Merely an Acceptance Mechanism.\n\n## 1. Ethical Refusal\n\nThe requested output should not be produced or used because doing so crosses a stated ethical boundary.\n\nName the principle, not simply discomfort.\n\n## 2. Positional Refusal\n\nThe problem is one you do not have standing to pursue. See [[#Module 2 — Problem-Finding|Module 2, Positionality]].\n\n## 3. Technical Refusal\n\nThe requested outcome cannot be reliably achieved under the available constraints.\n\nState:\n\n- what is technically blocking it\n- what alternatives exist\n- which trade-off is being accepted\n\n## 4. Intentional Refusal\n\nThe output may be technically possible and ethically permissible but would damage the premise, voice, or visual logic of the work.\n\nThis is a particularly important form of authorship.\n\n## Refusal Procedure\n\n1. Restate the request.\n2. Identify the underlying desired outcome.\n3. Diagnose the conflict.\n4. State the principle.\n5. Offer an alternative when appropriate.\n6. Record the decision.\n\n## Slop Refusal\n\nA practitioner may also reject work because it is generic in a way that undermines the project's intention.\n\nDistinguish:\n\n> \"I don't like this\"\n\nfrom:\n\n> \"This fails the project's defined criteria for specificity / focal hierarchy / voice / argument / distinctiveness.\"\n\n> [!example] What a Refusal Record Looks Like\n>\n> **Request:** Use a generated image of a real person's face in a commercial context without their consent.\n>\n> **Underlying outcome:** The client wants a recognizable face to lend credibility to the product.\n>\n> **Conflict:** Using a real person's likeness without consent violates their autonomy and may violate law.\n>\n> **Principle:** Do not generate identifiable people without consent.\n>\n> **Alternative:** Use a generated face that does not resemble any real person, or hire a model and obtain consent, or use a non-human image.\n>\n> **Decision:** Refuse the specific request. Offer the alternatives.\n>\n> **Result:** The client chose a generated face that did not resemble any real person. The campaign ran. The ethical boundary held.\n\n---"
