    "title": "Appendix H — Study Protocols for Building Creative Knowledge",
    "tags": [
      "craft",
      "study",
      "tacit"
    ],
    "summary": "Execution-as-knowledge drills: observational studies, close reading, and transcription.",
    "content": "> [!note] The Course Assumes That Judgment Needs a Growing Internal Reference System.\n\n## Visual Study Protocol 1 — Master Decomposition\n\n1. Select a strong image.\n2. Reduce to large masses.\n3. Identify focal hierarchy.\n4. Analyse value structure.\n5. Trace directional forces.\n6. Reconstruct underlying spatial forms.\n7. Reproduce the logic from memory.\n8. Compare.\n9. Record what was missed.\n10. Apply one discovered principle to a new image.\n\n## Visual Study Protocol 2 — Transformation\n\n1. Start with an observed object or figure.\n2. Construct it simply.\n3. Change viewpoint.\n4. Change proportion.\n5. Change gesture.\n6. Preserve identity.\n7. Invent an environment.\n8. Compare invented result with the original logic.\n\nThe purpose is to move from observation to controlled invention.\n\n## Visual Study Protocol 3 — Composition Extraction\n\nFor a master image:\n\n- ignore subject identity\n- identify masses\n- identify directional flow\n- identify contrast hierarchy\n- reconstruct using unrelated subject matter\n\nThis teaches mechanisms rather than imitation.\n\n## Visual Study Protocol 4 — Deliberate Deviation\n\n1. Select a convention (rule of thirds, symmetry, tonal contrast, edge hierarchy).\n2. State what the convention accomplishes.\n3. Produce three studies that violate it deliberately.\n4. For each, state what the violation achieves.\n5. Decide whether any of the violations serves a purpose you would pursue.\n\n## Writing Study Protocol 1 — Imitation\n\n1. Select a strong passage.\n2. Identify its structural moves.\n3. Rewrite the passage on a different subject while preserving the structural behaviour.\n4. Rewrite again without the original syntax.\n5. Compare.\n\n## Writing Study Protocol 2 — Compression\n\n1. Take 500 words.\n2. Reduce to 250.\n3. Reduce to 100.\n4. Reduce to 30.\n5. State what was lost at each stage.\n6. Restore only what serves the premise.\n\n## Writing Study Protocol 3 — Voice Separation\n\nWrite the same claim in:\n\n- your default voice\n- a neutral professional voice\n- a deliberately constrained voice\n- a voice derived from an analysed human anchor\n\nThen identify which mechanisms changed.\n\nThe purpose is to make voice controllable without reducing it to adjectives.\n\n## Writing Study Protocol 4 — Deliberate Deviation\n\n1. Select a convention (paragraph length, syntactic parallelism, resolution, closure).\n2. State what the convention accomplishes.\n3. Produce three short pieces that violate it deliberately.\n4. For each, state what the violation achieves.\n5. Decide whether any of the violations serves a purpose you would pursue.\n\n## Time-Based Study Protocol (Deepening)\n\n1. Select a short sequence (film scene, audio passage, performance fragment).\n2. Analyse pacing, silence, held frames, transitions.\n3. Reconstruct the logic.\n4. Produce a short sequence with the same durational logic, different content.\n5. Compare.\n\n---"
